00:28:09,340 --> 00:28:14,420
like, are we actually able to solve this problem, you know, in the future?

295
00:28:15,200 --> 00:28:21,150
Good question, and I think that's actually one of the reasons I find these multi -agent interaction problems to be very different.

296
00:28:21,200 --> 00:28:27,590
It's much harder to scale up data, and in single agent, when we have SIM to real transfer,

297
00:28:27,640 --> 00:28:33,310
we're like, oh, let's just randomize the simulation environment, hope that our policies transfer.

298
00:28:33,360 --> 00:28:37,420
But let's say you want to pair your policy with another policy that you have trained in simulation.

299
00:28:37,780 --> 00:28:44,970
Now there is another layer of robustness that you need to add to your policy, which is all the ways that another agent could do anything.

300
00:28:45,020 --> 00:28:47,330
And that's very hard to collect.

301
00:28:47,380 --> 00:28:49,260
And even if you collect a lot of data,

302
00:28:49,480 --> 00:28:58,420
like there are several aspects of like let's say cultural norms or conventions that play into what everybody's inherent in people's interactions.

303
00:28:58,580 --> 00:29:05,300
For example, one of the things that we found to be really hard is that if you use multi -agent reinforcement learning in simulation,

304
00:29:05,720 --> 00:29:12,180
getting it to work in hardware is much harder than single agent domain, because of the deviations that the other agents could have.

305
00:29:12,480 --> 00:29:17,270
So I think the question is, how can we even robustify policies?

306
00:29:17,320 --> 00:29:24,310
And I don't think relying just on scalable data collection is gonna be the answer, simply because it is not even scalable.

307
00:29:24,360 --> 00:29:27,720
And then you could argue, now I have two agents, what if I had another agent?

