00:33:24,720 --> 00:33:26,370
Hello everyone, I'm Chuan Xiong.

364
00:33:26,420 --> 00:33:29,070
Today I'd like to introduce our work with VLA.

365
00:33:29,120 --> 00:33:35,500
What if a robot policy could use weights of just minus one, zero, and plus one?

366
00:33:35,800 --> 00:33:40,520
This is BitWLA, the first fully native one -bit visual language action model.

367
00:33:40,760 --> 00:33:51,220
Compared with OpenWLA -OFT, BitWLA uses 11 times less memory and runs 4.4 times faster, with competitive manipulation performance.

368
00:33:51,500 --> 00:33:55,350
This makes BitWLA cost -effective for edge -deployed robots.

369
00:33:55,400 --> 00:34:01,100
We evaluate B2LA's performance and generalization on real -world tasks.

370
00:34:01,500 --> 00:34:08,680
At a similar model size, B2LA outperforms Pi0 and approaches the much larger OpenLA OFT.

371
00:34:09,120 --> 00:34:12,300
Let's take a look at some B2LA robot rollouts.

372
00:34:12,500 --> 00:34:18,540
The top row shows in distribution tasks, the bottom row tests unseen objects and backgrounds.

373
00:34:19,550 --> 00:34:23,740
Also, B2LA achieves strong performance on simulation benchmarks.

374
00:34:23,790 --> 00:34:26,780
So how do we achieve this?

375
00:34:26,830 --> 00:34:28,230
The key is bit -linear.

376
00:34:28,450 --> 00:34:36,790
It coincides with 1.58 bits and activations to INT8, then rescales the output back to BF16.

377
00:34:37,050 --> 00:34:41,290
We replace standard linear layers with it to reduce inference cost.

378
00:34:41,340 --> 00:34:44,660
We train B2LA in three stages.

379
00:34:44,900 --> 00:34:48,280
First, following a large -scale multimodal pre -training setup,

380
00:34:48,660 --> 00:34:55,400
we align the BitNet2B4T with the BF16YIT to establish strong visual language grounding.

381
00:34:55,720 --> 00:35:00,960
Next, we introduce a quantized and distilled stage to compress the YIT.

382
00:35:01,500 --> 00:35:11,680
An auxiliary distillation loss aligns the low -bit students' latent representations with the BF16YT teacher helping preserve accuracy.

383
00:35:12,160 --> 00:35:18,760
Finally, we perform robotics pro -training on the fully 1 -bit BLM and then fine -tune for downstream tasks.

384
00:35:19,380 --> 00:35:21,990
With 10 -row weights and in -the -head activations,

385
00:35:22,040 --> 00:35:28,320
B2LA dramatically reduces floating -point multiplier cumulate operations in linear transformations.

386
00:35:29,000 --> 00:35:35,710
This opens the door to specialize the accelerator designs for energy -efficient edge hardware.

387
00:35:35,760 --> 00:35:39,280
Efficient models also need efficient runtimes.

388
00:35:39,600 --> 00:35:48,000
You can run B2LA using robot.cpp, our official inference framework, which natively supports package ternary weights.

389
00:35:48,720 --> 00:35:51,940
So the GOF5 is just 1.44GB.

390
00:35:52,460 --> 00:35:58,210
On an RTS1390, B2LA runs 3.52 times faster than compared to PyTorch.

391
00:35:58,260 --> 00:36:06,520
And robot.cpp is not just for bitway, it also supports multiple models, operating systems, and backends.

392
00:36:06,800 --> 00:36:10,540
Our goal is to make deploying robot models as easy as possible.

393
00:36:10,740 --> 00:36:16,760
No complicated passing environment, no compilation, just download, launch, and run it.

394
00:36:17,220 --> 00:36:20,280
Check out our paper, code, model, and robot.cpp.

395
00:36:20,520 --> 00:36:22,850
Our poster is at number 59.

